  "termination": "completed",
  "answer": "Both writable files are updated.\n\n**What changed**\n\n- `registry.mjs` — `resolveFromRegistry` now implements the required policy:\n  - Validates the path first (`TypeError` for non-string, empty, or non-`/`-prefixed), so invalid input throws even when records are empty.\n  - Scans all records: the **first matching static** record returns immediately (static always beats dynamic, regardless of declaration order); the **first matching dynamic** is remembered; the **first fallback** is remembered.\n  - After the scan: dynamic match wins over fallback; fallback is used only if nothing matched; otherwise `null`.\n  - Returns fresh `{ route, params }` objects with a copied `params` object; records are never mutated, and no state is shared between calls.\n  - Matching is delegated to the existing `matchStatic`/`matchDynamic` helpers, which are exact/case-sensitive and treat trailing slashes as distinct (segment-count comparison), so pathname text is untouched.\n- `entry.mjs` — `dispatch(path)` no longer consults `legacyResolve`; it is now purely `resolveFromRegistry(path, defaultRoutes)`, so `/old/help` is no longer special-cased in the production entry point and invalid paths throw `TypeError`.\n\n**Unchanged:** `legacy.mjs` (still returns `legacy-help` for `/old/help`), the adapters, utils, route helpers, and fixtures.\n\n**Checked:** I re-read the final `registry.mjs` and reviewed the logic against the fixture ordering (`/users/:id` before `/users/new`, fallback in the middle) — `/users/new` → `user-new`, `/users/Ada` → `user-detail` with `{id:'Ada'}`, `/missing` → `not-found`, `/health` → `health`. No tests were executed (no shell/test runner available), so nothing is claimed as passing.",
